<section id="about" class="about shell">
  <div class="edge-pad about-grid">
    <div class="about-index">
      <span class="section-label">01 / About</span>
      <span class="mono big-index" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
    </div>
    <div class="about-body">
      <p class="lede">
        Three threads run through what I build: models that touch real
        patients, systems that make AI teams faster, and the standing
        question of whether either can be trusted.
      </p>
      <p>
        At Emory, I split my time between coursework in information
        retrieval and AI systems and a research project probing how easily
        diffusion models trained on tabular genomics data leak the patients
        inside them — the same models increasingly used to synthesize
        medical datasets that are assumed to be safe. Before that, at TCS,
        I replaced a 100-person team's 24–48 hour manual review process
        with a serverless multi-agent pipeline on AWS — the unglamorous,
        load-bearing kind of AI work that doesn't make headlines but has to
        actually ship, on someone else's production timeline.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm drawn to problems that sit at the boundary of the model and the
        system around it, where accuracy on a benchmark matters less than
        whether the thing can be trusted with real data, in production,
        under someone else's SLA.
      </p>
      <div class="focus-tags">
        <span class="tag">Applied ML</span>
        <span class="tag">Privacy &amp; Membership Inference</span>
        <span class="tag">Medical Imaging</span>
        <span class="tag">Serverless / AWS</span>
        <span class="tag">Agentic Systems</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="rule"></div>
</section>

